    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Book of Kells was made around 800 by Columban monks, and its fully developed ornament places it late in the series of Insular Gospel books.</li><li>Its decoration is far more lavish than that of any other surviving Insular Gospel book, yet the text has uncorrected mistakes and unusable canon tables, which suggests that its look mattered more than its practical use.</li><li>Stolen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> in 1007 and recovered under a sod, it was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 1654 and given to Trinity College in 1661, where it has remained.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Modern examination of the pigments showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">Murex</a> shell purple and lapis lazuli, long thought to have been used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, were not; the woad and lichen used for the blues and purples are strong evidence of an origin in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

