    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 800</span><span class="rf-text">The Book of Kells was created in a Columban monastery in Ireland or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">814</span><span class="rf-text">The church of the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> was consecrated after building had begun in 807</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1007</span><span class="rf-text">The great Gospel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> was stolen from the western sacristy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> and later recovered under a sod, without its golden cover</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1654</span><span class="rf-text">The governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> sent the book to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> for safekeeping when Cromwell's cavalry was quartered in the church</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1661</span><span class="rf-text">Henry Jones presented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1953</span><span class="rf-text">Roger Powell rebound the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in four volumes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2011</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added the book to its Memory of the World International Register</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels.jpg" alt="Lindisfarne Gospels" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels">Lindisfarne Gospels</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LindisfarneFol27rIncipitMatt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eadfrith of Lindisfarne (presumed), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Book of Kells belongs to a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in the Insular style, produced from the late 6th through the early 9th centuries in monasteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland and in continental monasteries with Hiberno-Scottish or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> foundations. Earlier members include the Cathach of St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> and the Book of Durrow; the early 8th century brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels">Lindisfarne Gospels</a>, the Echternach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield-gospels">Lichfield Gospels</a>, and the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> is dated to 807–809. Scholars group these manuscripts by similarities in artistic style, script and textual tradition, and the fully developed ornament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> places it late in the series, in the late 8th or early 9th century.</p><p>The Abbey of Kells in County Meath had been founded, or refounded, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> Abbey, with building running from 807 until the church was consecrated in 814. The manuscript's date and place of production have been much debated. Tradition held that it was made in the time of Columba, perhaps by his own hand, but this has long been discredited on palaeographic and stylistic grounds: most evidence points to a date around 800, long after Columba's death in 597. The proposed 9th-century dating coincides with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> and Iona, which began around 793–794 and eventually dispersed the monks and their relics into Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Another tradition, with some traction among Irish scholars, holds that it was made for the 200th anniversary of the saint's death.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Book of Kells&#8221; cites where it covers Origin, Medieval period:</p><ul><li>Martyn Lyons, Books: A Living History (J. Paul Getty Museum, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jesus.jpg" alt="Jesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spas_vsederzhitel_sinay_(cropped1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Book of Kells contains the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, preceded by prefaces, summaries and concordances. The surviving book has preliminary matter, the complete Gospels of Matthew, Mark and Luke, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a> as far as John 17:13. The preliminaries are two fragmentary lists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> names, the Breves causae (Gospel summaries), the Argumenta (short biographies of the Evangelists) and the Eusebian canon tables, which were developed to cross-reference the Gospels. The text is largely Vulgate, with several passages from the earlier Old Latin versions, and it differs from the Vulgate in places: at Matthew 10:34 it reads gaudium ("joy") where gladium ("sword") is expected.</p><p>The text is accompanied by ten full-page miniatures: the Virgin and Child, three pages of evangelist symbols, two evangelist portraits, Christ enthroned, a carpet page, the Arrest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and the Temptation of Christ. Thirty-three of the surviving pages carry decoration that dominates the whole page. The opening words of each Gospel are decorated so lavishly that the text is almost illegible, and the Chi Rho monogram at Matthew 1:18 grows to consume an entire page, with animals and insects hidden in its swirling knotwork and three angels rising from one arm of the chi. It is the largest and most lavish Chi Rho in any Insular Gospel book. Peacocks appear as symbols of Christ, because the flesh of the peacock was held not to putrefy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The book must have been the product of a major scriptorium over several years, yet it was apparently never finished: the planned decoration of some pages appears only in outline. As many as twelve individuals may have collabourated, of whom four scribes and three painters have been distinguished. Françoise Henry identified three scribes, Hand A, Hand B and Hand C, and a fourth, Hand D, has been hypothesised. She also named three artists: the "Goldsmith", who made the Chi Rho page, the "Illustrator", who produced the Temptation and the Arrest of Christ, and the "Portrait Painter", who painted Christ and the Evangelists. The script is Insular majuscule, written in iron gall ink on calf vellum of high quality but uneven thickness.</p><p>The palette is broad, with purple, lilac, red, pink, green and yellow the commonest colours, whereas the Book of Durrow uses only four. As is usual in Insular work, no gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> leaf was used. The pigments included red and yellow ochre, a green <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> pigment, orcein and woad, and the yellow orpiment was exported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The scribes seem often to have depended on memory rather than on their exemplar, and the order of the Breves causae and Argumenta repeats that of the Book of Durrow, which led T. K. Abbott to conclude that the scribes had Durrow or a common model in hand. Four competing theories place the work at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a>, at Iona alone, at Kells alone, or at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld">Dunkeld</a> or another Pictish monastery; the first is widely accepted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/queen-victoria.jpg" alt="Queen Victoria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Victoria_by_Bassano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexander Bassano, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The book had a sacramental rather than an educational purpose. A Gospel so large and lavish would have been left on the high altar and removed only for the reading of the Gospel at Mass, with the reader probably reciting from memory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> record that it was stolen from the sacristy, where the vessels of the Mass were kept, not from a library. The design seems to reflect this: numerous mistakes were left uncorrected, lines were often finished in a blank space in the line above, and the chapter numbers that would have made the canon tables usable were never put into the margins, so that aesthetics were given priority over utility.</p><p>In the 12th century Gerald of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> described seeing a great Gospel book in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a>, which many have since assumed was the Book of Kells, writing that "you might say that all this was the work of an angel, and not of a man". Land charters of the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> were copied onto its blank pages in the 12th century. Its fame as a world treasure began in the 19th century, when its association with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> was used to demonstrate Ireland's cultural primacy in the debate over the relative authority of the Irish and Roman churches. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and Prince Albert were invited to sign it in 1849, and its artistry influenced the Celtic Revival.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster.jpg" alt="Annals of Ulster" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annals-of-ulster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> Abbey was pillaged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> many times at the beginning of the 9th century, and how the book survived is not known. The earliest historical reference is a 1007 entry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a>, which records that the great Gospel of Columkille was stolen at night from the western sacristy of the great stone church at Cenannas on account of its wrought shrine, and recovered a few months later, without its golden and bejewelled cover, "under a sod". The force of tearing it from its cover may account for the folios missing from the beginning and end. The abbey was dissolved because of the ecclesiastical reforms of the 12th century, and its church became a parish church in which the book remained.</p><p>In 1654, when Cromwell's cavalry was quartered in the church, the governor of the town sent the book to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> for safekeeping, and in 1661 Henry Jones, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Clogher, presented it to Trinity College. About 370 folios are believed to have existed originally; James Ussher counted 344 in 1621, and the missing bifolium 335–336 was found and restored in 1741. A 19th-century rebinding badly cropped the pages and cut off small parts of some illustrations, a rebinding of 1895 broke down quickly, and in 1953 Roger Powell rebound the book in four volumes. In 2000 the volume holding Mark suffered what has been called minor pigment damage on its way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a>, perhaps from the vibration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">aeroplane</a>'s engines.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 19th century several Victorian picture books of medieval illuminations featured designs from the Book of Kells, which were copied and adapted extensively, so that its patterns appeared in metalwork, embroidery, furniture and pottery. Helen Campbell D'Olier made some of the first faithful reproductions, on vellum with pigments like the original, and photographs of her drawings appeared in Sullivan's study of 1913. The Swiss publisher Urs Graf Verlag Bern produced the first facsimile in 1951, Thames and Hudson a partial facsimile in 1974 with a study by Françoise Henry, and Faksimile-Verlag Luzern a full-colour facsimile published in 1990, made after a process using gentle suction allowed pages to be photographed without being touched.</p><p>Trinity College produced a digital copy in 2006, and the Faksimile-Verlag images are now online in its Digital Collections portal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is shown in Trinity College Library, two pages at a time, rotated every six to eight weeks. Christopher de Hamel writes that the Ireland in which it was made "was clearly no primitive backwater", and that it is "as uniquely Irish as anything imaginable" while also being a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> text, with lines of trade and communication unknown to us. In 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added it to its Memory of the World International Register, recognising it as a globally important historical document.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Book of Kells is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Gospel book made around 800 by Columban monks, with ten full-page miniatures and ornament more lavish than in any other surviving Insular Gospel book. Stolen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> in 1007 and recovered, it went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> in 1661 and was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World Register</a> in 2011.</em></p>

